                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "The game does what it set out to do very well.\n\nIt does not however try to do anything beyond its intended scope just to try please everyone, and there will always be daft twits that see \"Ship Design Corporation Simulator\" and react with \"Oh I liked Faster Than Light! But this game doesn't seem to be remotely as fun. What a shame, clearly a failing of the developers capability.\".\n\nIf the games actual design concept appeals to you of managing your own ship design company where you take on contracts with functional requirements you have to make sure your designs meet and putting your designs through simulated tests to make sure it performs as required. Then turning around and reinvesting your money into R&D and expanding your business empire, you'll very much enjoy this game. \n\nIt was already heavily enjoyable in the early builds of early access. And since then the developer has gone to great lengths to listen to feedback and make changes or add additional options into the game that they never intended to make until the community spoke up.\n\nFor example the test auto-resolves? Never intended to be a feature in the game as the developer personally really enjoyed the RTS element, but when early access first hit there was a lot of feedback from the community about not enjoying the RTS side of design testing and the desire to have a automated way to measure a ships performance without having to go through drawn out RTS scenarios each time. And despite the developer feeling the RTS aspect was fun, enjoyable and exactly what the game needed to make it more fun, they still listened to the feedback and invested development time into the auto-resolve option as well as additional development time later on addressing bugs and undesired results in the auto-resolve behaviour, so those that wanted to focus more on the business management and ship design portion of the game could do so.\n\n\nBut that's entirely what this game is. It's a Business management and ship design simulator with some now optional RTS crew management scenarios. It's not \"Faster Than Light Inc.\", \"Freelancer & Sons\" or any kind of mixture of RTS-Space Combat-Rogue-like-With-MineCraft-Ship-Building or some nonsense like that..... and I get the feeling some people expect this game to be something entirely different to what it was intended to be.\n\nAnd I guess I can see why some people would be expecting something different. Especially if they're the type of person who relates things based on other examples... because there's really nothing else out there like Starship Corporation. \n\nSo if you're the type of person who relates to things like \"It's like FTL but with bits of Metroid in it, in the style of Hollow Knight and some sprinkling of Call of Duty\", then Starship Corporation is going to be an anomaly for you and you'll probably have a hard time getting to grips with what the game actually is and what it is not.\n\nHowever If you are sold on the idea of what the game is offering, and just what the game is offering, then this game will almost definitely be an enjoyable experience for you with a lot of hours to invest in if you want to dedicate yourself toward just 1 game.\n\nOn the other hand if the idea of the game doesn't sound appealing to you, just stop immediately. Don't start trying to create some imaginary idea of what the game might actually be beyond what it's offering, buy the game and then blame the game (or the developer) for not meeting your imaginary rainbow unicorn idea. Just click away and don't buy this game. You are unlikely to enjoy it as it has a very specific target audience.",
